sections.content:
**Modular arithmetic** treats numbers as if they wrap around at a modulus `m`. `a mod m` is the remainder when `a` is divided by `m`.

## Key Identities

```
(a + b) % m = ((a % m) + (b % m)) % m
(a * b) % m = ((a % m) * (b % m)) % m
(a - b) % m = ((a % m) - (b % m) + m) % m   // +m to handle negatives
```

Division is NOT this simple - you need modular inverse (see Variants).

## Why We Mod

Numbers in CP can exceed 10^18. JavaScript's safe integer limit is 2^53 - 1 ≈ 9 × 10^15. Products of two 10^9 numbers overflow. By taking mod at every step, we keep intermediate values small.

## Modular Exponentiation (Fast Power)

Computing `a^n % m` naively with a loop is O(n). **Fast power** uses repeated squaring: `a^n = (a^(n/2))^2` if n is even, `a * a^(n-1)` if n is odd. This is O(log n).

```javascript
function modPow(base, exp, mod) {
  let result = 1n;
  base = BigInt(base) % BigInt(mod);
  exp = BigInt(exp);
  mod = BigInt(mod);
  while (exp > 0n) {
    if (exp % 2n === 1n) result = result * base % mod;
    base = base * base % mod;
    exp >>= 1n;
  }
  return Number(result);
}
```

A **prime** is a number greater than 1 with no divisors other than 1 and itself. Note: **1 is NOT prime**.

## Trial Division - O(√n)

To check if `n` is prime, try dividing by all integers from 2 to √n. If none divide evenly, it's prime. We stop at √n because if `n = a * b` and `a > √n`, then `b < √n` - the smaller factor would have been found already.

```javascript
function isPrime(n) {
  if (n < 2) return false;
  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i === 0) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Sieve of Eratosthenes - O(n log log n)

To find **all primes up to n**, use the Sieve. Mark all multiples of each prime as composite, starting from 2. What remains unmarked is prime.

```javascript
function sieve(n) {
  const isPrime = new Array(n + 1).fill(true);
  isPrime[0] = isPrime[1] = false;
  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (isPrime[i]) {
      for (let j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
        isPrime[j] = false;
      }
    }
  }
  return isPrime;
}
```

Use trial division for a single number. Use the sieve when you need all primes in a range.

